audio: back to Linux-owned I2S, and playback takes the whole SRAM pool

Copy esp32s31_generic.dts over linux-71-port/arch/riscv/boot/dts/espressif/
and esp32s31-i2s.c over linux-71-port/sound/soc/espressif/ before building.
A DTS change needs BOTH `make linux` AND `make opensbi`.

WHY THE HART0 PATH WAS ABANDONED
It worked - playback, sink switching, stored volume, resampling at every rate,
zero underruns - but it never sounded right. The wire rate has to be FIXED for
hart0 to mix, and 44100 is the rate this board has always crackled at, so
every source then converted to a rate that crackles. Linux-owned I2S takes
8000-96000 NATIVELY, so nothing converts at all, which is why it sounds clean.
That is the whole trade and it is not close.

WHAT CAME BACK WITH IT
  * loader: CONFIG_S31_AUDIO_ENABLE off (43 kB smaller)
  * dts: i2s0 okay, freertos_audio disabled, card cpu dai = &i2s0
  * audio_sram node re-enabled - it is Linux's DMA ring again
  * asound.conf, s31route and lvdesk's sink name all reverted

NO CAPTURE, AND THAT IS WHAT PAID FOR THE BUFFER
ESP32S31_I2S_BUFFER_BYTES feeds ONE snd_pcm_hardware shared by both streams,
so two streams meant two rings and the 64 KiB pool had to be split. Recording
is not wanted here, so the capture stream is gone from the DAI and playback
takes the entire pool: 32768 -> 65536 bytes, 186 -> 372 ms at 44100, about
1.5 s at 11025. No extra memory from hart0's heap. The RX plumbing is left
intact unopened; restoring capture is re-adding one block and halving this.

VERIFIED BY EAR: "sounds great".

--- CAPTURE STAYS, and why the buffer did not grow ---

Removing the capture stream to give playback the whole 64 KiB pool produced a
HIGH HISS and hollow, distant playback. The codec is a duplex part and with no
capture stream on the link its ADC path is never configured. Reverted:
capture is back in the DAI and ESP32S31_I2S_BUFFER_BYTES is 32768 again.

Taking that half is still possible, but the ADC has to be silenced first - see
/etc/init.d/S38audio-mixer and the lvdesk mixer fix - and then re-tested by
ear, not by counters.

--- THE HISS WAS TWO FAULTS, NOT ONE ---

1. lvdesk drove ADC2DAC Mixer. It selected EVERY mixer element with a playback
   volume and set them all to the user's volume; the ES8389 exposes its
   ADC->DAC SIDETONE as one. So every lvdesk start turned the microphone into
   the speaker at the slider's setting. Long predates any hart0 work. Fixed in
   lvdesk: prefer elements naming themselves a DAC, never one naming ADC.

2. A LATCHED ANALOG STATE in the codec, cleared only by a POWER CYCLE. Before
   it: silence at 44100 and 11025 both hissed. After it, nothing else changed:
   silence at 44100 and 48000 both clean, and Doom sounds right. Almost
   certainly caused here - hart0 spent a period clocking I2S onto the ES8311's
   default pads (52/53/55/56/54) instead of this board's (2/3/4/5/6) while also
   asserting the PA on GPIO7, and two ASRC attempts wedged hart0 mid-stream.

   THE LESSON IS THE ONE ALREADY IN CLAUDE.md AND IT WAS STILL MISSED: an
   esptool reset pulls the SoC's EN line only. The codec and amplifier have
   their own power domain and survive it. Hours went into software theories -
   digital attenuation into a fixed-gain amp, a wrong coefficient row for the
   clock ratio - for a fault that a power cycle had already fixed. When audio
   misbehaves after ANY experiment that drove I2S or the codec, power cycle
   FIRST and re-measure before theorising.
